            <ArticleTitle>[Ogilvie's syndrome: a rare cause of the acute abdomen].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>177-80</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Ogilvie's syndrome (acute colonic pseudo-obstruction) is a rare clinical disease characterized by segmental distension of the proximal colon caused by a paralysis without mechanic obstruction. It may be a sequel of underlying neurological, medical or surgical disease. Risk factors are respiratory decompensation, electrolyte disturbances and different drugs. A special kind is the primary idiopathic pseudoobstruction with a high risk of perforation or necrosis. Especially elderly patients (&gt; 70 years) with cardiovascular or neurologic diseases and accordant drugs are concerned. Clinical symptoms are progressive abdominal distension and abdominal pain like an acute abdomen. The differential diagnosis of a mechanic ileus is important for further treatment. This case report should draw attention to this rare disease.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
